# Error Handling

## Catch Blocks
Never use `catch(e: any)`. Always narrow with `instanceof`:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
} catch (e: any) {
  showAlert({ title: 'Error', message: e.message });
}

// ✅ GOOD
} catch (e) {
  showAlert({
    title: 'Operation Failed',
    message: e instanceof Error ? e.message : 'Unknown error',
  });
}
```

## User-Facing Errors
- Use `showAlert()` from the `useAlert` hook — never `Alert.alert` or `window.alert`
- Use thematic titles: "Operation Failed", "Access Denied", "Connection Error" (not generic "Error")
- Log to console only in dev: `if (__DEV__) console.error(e);`

## Service Layer
- Throw `new Error('descriptive message')` from `gameService.ts` / `taskService.ts`
- Let the calling screen component catch and display via `showAlert`
- Never swallow errors silently (no empty catch blocks)

## Error Boundary
The root `_layout.tsx` wraps the app in `<ErrorBoundary>`. For new route segments that could fail independently, wrap them in their own `<ErrorBoundary fallbackMessage="...">`.
